@startuml llm-orchestration-append
!theme plain
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam maxMessageSize 200
skinparam wrapWidth 180
title Simplest use case — "append" (send a question, stream the answer)\nTARGET structure: request builder → orchestrator → transport → evaluator → policy → postprocessor

actor User
participant "ChatNodeComponent\nsend()" as UI
participant "RequestBuilder" as BUILDER
participant "LlmOrchestrator\nrun(intent)" as ORCH
participant "LlmTransport\naskLlm()" as XPORT
participant "LlmLogService" as LOG
participant "ResponseEvaluator\n(completion)" as EVAL
participant "UsecasePolicy\n(append)" as POLICY
participant "Postprocessor\n(place)" as POST
participant "ChatService" as SVC
participant "Provider\n(proxy → /chat/completions SSE)" as NET

== 1. Request builder: intent from (chat, node, usecase, template vars) ==
User -> UI : Send (direction text)
UI -> UI : resolveSendTarget() — model + provider for the node/chat
UI -> BUILDER : build({ chat, node, usecase:'append',\nvars:{ sourceText, stream:true } })
note over BUILDER
  Fully generic: it only knows how to turn
  a use-case descriptor + template variables
  into request INTENTS. No UX, no placement.
end note
BUILDER --> UI : RequestIntent { usecase:'append', model,\nprovider, endpoint:'completion',\nmessages: contextPath + current node, stream:true }

== 2. Orchestrator: one generic loop ==
UI -> ORCH : run(intent, { onText: live chunks })
ORCH -> XPORT : send(intent)
XPORT -> LOG : record({ kind:'chat', body: full payload })
XPORT -> NET : POST /chat/completions (stream: true)

== 3. Transport streams + log attribution ==
loop each SSE chunk
  NET --> XPORT : chunk { content, thinking }
  XPORT --> ORCH : onText(chunk) — raw, unpaced
  ORCH --> POLICY : pipe chunk to target (chat-node bubble)\n(via onText callback)
  note over XPORT, LOG
    LlmLogService already completes the entry\nwith the response/error at the end of the call.
  end note
end
NET --> XPORT : final response
XPORT -> LOG : complete(entry, { response })

== 4. Evaluator: pure, never throws, fills slots ==
XPORT --> ORCH : { content, thinking }
ORCH -> EVAL : evaluate({ raw: final, error: undefined, aborted:false })
note over EVAL
  Generic completion evaluator.
  Fills ONE named slot:
    text  = Slot<{ content, thinking }>(status:'ok')
  On error/abort it would fill
    text = Slot(status:'error'|'refused', reason)
  — the same ORCH code path, no branching here.
end note
EVAL --> ORCH : EvalSlots { text }

== 5. Policy: read slots → no continuation → place ==
ORCH -> POLICY : nextIntents(slots)
POLICY --> ORCH : []  (append is a terminal use case)
ORCH -> POLICY : place(slots, intent)
POLICY -> POST : place({ usecase:'append', slots:{ text } })

== 6. Postprocessor: consistent chat handling ==
POST -> SVC : persistQuestion(chat, node, text)  (user node saved)
POST -> SVC : addNode(role:assistant, content:'') — placeholder bubble
POST -> SVC : updateNodes(content …) — live streaming reveal
POST -> SVC : editAssistant(final content + thinking)
POST -> SVC : setActiveChild(question → answer)
SVC --> POST : saved assistant node
POST --> UI : activate(answer.id) → scroll into view
note over POST, SVC
  Postprocessor is the ONLY consumer-facing\nplace that writes chat state.
end note

@enduml